oimaging often showed a hypoplastic corpus callosum and posterior-predominant white-matter changes. In one individual, gray matter heterotopias were also observed."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"heterozygous de novo variant"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:41858232:1","pmid":"41858232","section":"nomenclature","statement":"Galaz-Montoya et al. (2026) proposed the descriptive term 'autosomal dominant FBXO31-associated neurodevelopmental disorder' and the eponym 'Kruer syndrome' for the phenotype caused by the recurrent de novo FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn variant.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["We propose the descriptive term \"autosomal dominant FBXO31-associated neurodevelopmental disorder,\" and-consistent with the validating laboratory and with support from the FBXO31 Foundation-propose the eponym \"Kruer syndrome.\""],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:41858232:2","pmid":"41858232","section":"diagnosis","statement":"Galaz-Montoya et al. (2026) concluded that recurrent FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn variants cause a recognizable neurodevelopmental syndrome. They recommended including FBXO31 in diagnostic algorithms for cerebral palsy and neurodevelopmental disorders.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Recurrent FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn variants lead to a recognizable neurodevelopmental syndrome. Based on our findings, we recommend including FBXO31 in diagnostic algorithms for cerebral palsy and neurodevelopmental disorders."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:41858232:3","pmid":"41858232","section":"heterogeneity","statement":"Galaz-Montoya et al. (2026) noted that biallelic loss-of-function variants in FBXO31 cause autosomal recessive intellectual disability. In contrast, the recurrent de novo c.1000G>A (p.Asp334Asn) variant is associated with an autosomal dominant phenotype.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Biallelic loss-of-function variants in FBXO31 cause autosomal-recessive intellectual disability. A recurrent de novo variant, c.1000G>A(p.Asp334Asn), has been described in association with an autosomal-dominant phenotype."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:33675180:1","pmid":"33675180","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Dzinovic et al. (2021) compared their patient with the 2 previously reported patients. All 3 had motor and/or speech delay, mild to moderate intellectual disability, abnormal speech, and spasticity that mainly affected the lower limbs and led to a diagnosis of cerebral palsy in each case. Dystonia was seen only in their patient.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["The three cases shared a presentation of motor and/or speech delay, and all had ID that varied from mild to moderate.","The latter comprised spasticity (as seen in all three patients), predominantly affecting the lower limbs and leading to a diagnosis of CP in each subject, as well as dystonia (observed in the herein reported case)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:33675180:2","pmid":"33675180","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Dzinovic et al. (2021) found the variant absent from control databases. It changed a conserved residue in a domain that interacts with the FBXO31 substrate cyclin D1, and they classified it as pathogenic under ACMG guidelines. They found no other potentially disease-relevant variants in the exome.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["This variant, absent from control databases (gnomAD, in‐house exomes), has been described recently in two individuals with an NDD diagnosed as spastic diplegic CP.","The variant affected a conserved residue within a functionally important domain of the encoded protein, involved in the interaction with FBXO31`s substrate cyclin‐D1 (Fig. 1A and B).","No other potentially disease‐relevant variants were observed in the proband`s WES data."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:33675180:3","pmid":"33675180","section":"description","statement":"Dzinovic et al. (2021) suggested that emerging hallmarks of FBXO31-related disease include delayed psychomotor development, pyramidal and extrapyramidal dysfunction (spasticity and dystonia), impaired expressive and receptive language, and intellectual disability. They recommended considering de novo FBXO31 mutation in patients with cerebral palsy or spastic-dystonic disorders that have no other explanation.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Emerging hallmarks of FBXO31‐related disease seem to include a delay in psychomotor development, dysfunction of both pyramidal and extrapyramidal systems (as evidenced by the manifestation of spasticity and dystonia), impairments in expressive and receptive language, and ID.","The occurrence of de novo FBXO31 mutation should be considered in patients with CP and spastic‐dystonic disorders for which no alternative cause is found."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:32989326:1","pmid":"32989326","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"In a whole-exome sequencing study of 250 cerebral palsy parent-offspring trios, Jin et al. (2020) identified an identical de novo FBXO31 variant (D334N) in 2 unrelated patients with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, an unlikely chance event identifying FBXO31 as a novel monogenic etiology.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["We identified two novel monogenic etiologies, FBXO31 and RHOB, and showed that the RHOB mutation enhances active-state Rho effector binding while the FBXO31 mutation diminishes cyclin D levels.","We also identified two unrelated cases with an identical DNM (encoding p.Asp334Asn; Fig. 2a and Supplementary Table 4) in FBXO31"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:32989326:2","pmid":"32989326","section":"gene_function","statement":"Jin et al. (2020) found that the FBXO31 D334N variant lies at a conserved residue within the cyclin D1-binding pocket, and patient fibroblasts showed reduced cyclin D levels compared to controls, consistent with an apparent gain of function in cyclin D degradation.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn affects the cyclin D interaction site (Fig. 2b), leading to an apparent gain of function of cyclin D degradation (Fig. 2c).","both patients had reduced cyclin D compared to pooled controls. the data are averaged for three independent cell culture experiments (n = 7 controls, n = 6 patient measurements)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:32989326:3","pmid":"32989326","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Jin et al. (2020) concluded that the de novo heterozygous FBXO31 D334N variant leads to a phenotype distinct from the nonsyndromic intellectual disability associated with homozygous truncating FBXO31 mutations, representing a phenotypic expansion.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Therefore, this DNM in FBXO31 leads to a phenotype distinct from the previously described autosomal recessive truncating mutation-associated non-syndromic ID phenotype.","In other cases, typified by FBXO31, our findings likely represent phenotypic expansions."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["FBXO31"],"confirmed":["FBXO31"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AD"]}